# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The project virtualenv (python-docx, pypdf, Pillow, pypdfium2 - see requirements.txt).
# Recreate it with:
#   python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt
.venv/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# playwright-core is the only runtime dependency; the lock file, if you keep one, stays tracked.
node_modules/
.npm-cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch space and local cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tmp/ holds ad-hoc probes, DOM dumps, render QA directories and run logs.
# .cache/ holds the Chrome CDP profile and a project-local LibreOffice, if installed.
tmp/
.cache/

# One-off probes left in the tree by hand.
debug_*.mjs
*_probe*.mjs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# output/ is one directory for everything generated, split by type:
#   output/pdf/            final resumes + cover letters that get uploaded   -> tracked
#   output/screen/         screening reports (daily_*, newgrad_screen_*)     -> tracked
#   output/seen_jobs.json  the screener's own dedupe state                   -> tracked
#   output/metrics/        the append-only ledger and its reports            -> ignored
#   output/docx/           intermediate resumes                              -> ignored
#   output/jd/             scraped job-description text                      -> ignored
#   output/<run>/          per-run fill results and screenshots              -> ignored
#   output/logs/           run logs                                          -> ignored
# THE WHOLE DIRECTORY, AND THIS DEPARTS FROM THE CHECKOUT THIS WAS EXTRACTED FROM ON PURPOSE. There,
# `output/pdf/` and `output/screen/` were deliberately TRACKED - versioning one person's finished resumes
# and screening reports was useful to them. In a repository meant to be published, the same setting
# publishes those resumes: a tailored PDF carries the applicant's name, address, phone, employers and
# history, and the screening reports carry the postings they were considering. Generated output is not
# source, and none of it belongs in a public history.
output/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store

# ===========================================================================
# PERSONAL DATA - NEVER COMMIT ANY OF THIS
# ===========================================================================
# This toolkit is built to be pointed at one real person: the config files under
# data/ hold that person's identity, work history, eligibility answers and the
# record of every job they applied to. Copying the examples in `examples/` into
# `data/` is the setup step, and it is also the moment this repository stops
# being generic.
#
# Do not commit them:
#
#   data/candidate_profile.json    name, email, phone, links, standing answers,
#                                  confirmed skills, approved metrics, do_not_claim
#   data/application_profile.json  address, education, work history, Workday defaults
#   data/applications.csv          the application tracker: every job applied to
#   data/form_specs/               per-application fill specs - they contain your answers
#   data/application_bundles/      per-application manifests
#   data/cover_letter/template.txt your own letter wording, filled with your own facts
#   data/tenant_policies.json      what individual employers told you, including rejections
#   data/tenant_caps.json          the application limits you hit, and how you found out
#
# They are ignored below as a safety net. `git add -f` still overrides it, so the
# ignore file is not the real protection: the real protection is never running a
# blanket `git add -A` in a directory that holds answers about a real person.
# A leaked profile is not a code leak - it is a stranger holding your address, your
# phone number, your visa status and your salary expectation.
#
# THE WHOLE DIRECTORY, not a list of the paths that hold an answer today. This was an enumeration, and it
# fell behind the code three times: `data/resume_templates/` (your master DOCX - the resume itself),
# `data/screening/sources.json` (the boards you watch), `data/autofill/` (the per-job configs carrying
# your name, email and phone) and `data/chrome-cdp-profile/` (a browser profile) were all outside it. An
# enumeration is only ever as complete as the day it was written, and the thing it fails to cover is
# whatever was added last - which is exactly the file nobody has checked.
#
# `data/` holds nothing but your configuration, and everything it is built from lives in `examples/`.
# There is no file under it this repository means to ship, so there is nothing to name individually.
data/

# THE PER-PLATFORM CONTROL MAPS UNDER `skills/*/references/` ARE NOT IGNORED, deliberately. They are
# instruction material the toolkit resolves from inside the checkout rather than user configuration, so a
# fork that writes its own wants to commit them. The one thing that writes there is the contract fixture
# runner, which installs invented ones for a single run and removes them on every exit path - and which
# refuses to run at all when the directory already holds yours.

# --------------------------------------------------- the contract test fixture runner
# `npm run test:contract` writes its fabricated configuration into a temporary data directory that
# `ATS_DATA_DIR` points at - never into this checkout's `data/` - so the only thing it puts inside the
# repository is the per-platform control maps under `skills/*/references/`, installed for one run and
# removed from one list on every exit path. Listing that path here means a crash or an interrupted run
# cannot leave fabricated notes looking like shipped documentation.
